NAME
       su - run a shell or a command as another user

SYNOPSIS
       su [-] [-l] [-c COMMAND] [-s SHELL] [-m] [USER]

DESCRIPTION
       su (switch user) starts a shell as USER, or as root when no user is
       given. It asks for the password of the target account, not your own;
       root may switch to any account without a password. Type exit to go
       back to the shell you came from.

       There are two kinds of switch, and the difference matters:

       su USER
              Changes only the identity. You keep your current directory and
              most of your environment, including PATH. Programs may then
              look for files in the wrong places.

       su - USER  (or su -l USER)
              Starts a login shell, as if USER had logged in: it goes to
              USER's home directory, sets HOME, SHELL, USER, LOGNAME and PATH
              for USER and runs the login scripts (/etc/profile,
              ~/.bash_profile). This is almost always the one you want.

       Compared with sudo, su needs the target account's password and gives a
       whole shell; sudo needs your own password, checks the sudoers rules
       and logs each command.

OPTIONS
       -, -l, --login
              Start a login shell (see above). Put it before the user name.

       -c, --command=COMMAND
              Run only COMMAND (one argument: quote it) as the user, then come
              back.

       -s, --shell=SHELL
              Run SHELL instead of the user's shell from /etc/passwd.

       -m, -p, --preserve-environment
              Keep the current environment.

EXAMPLES
       Become root with root's own environment:

           su -

       Become the user student (from root, no password is asked):

           su - student

       Run one command as student and return to root:

           su - student -c 'crontab -l'

       Check what a user can reach, as that user:

           su - student -c 'ls -l /srv/shared'

NOTES
       An account whose shell is /sbin/nologin cannot get a shell with su;
       it prints "This account is currently not available.".

       Logging in as root over ssh may be refused while su - still works
       from a normal account, depending on PermitRootLogin in sshd_config.

FILES
       /etc/pam.d/su
              The authentication rules for su.

SEE ALSO
       sudo(8), login(1), bash(1), passwd(5)
